---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
---

<Article
  title="Published responses"
  description="A guide to the statements, technical work, published guidance, warnings, disclosure disputes and legal documents preserved after the COLDCARD entropy incident."
  standfirst="A guide to what organisations and individuals published after the disclosure. Statements, code proposals, guidance, warnings and disputed accounts are kept in separate parts of the record so that one does not stand in for another."
  depth="plain"
  kicker="Responses"
  next={{
    href: '/response/statements/',
    label: 'Public statements',
    note: 'What Coinkite, custody providers, wallet vendors and community organisations published.',
  }}
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      The public response was not one story. It included statements from
      Coinkite and other vendors, proposed code changes, migration guidance,
      scam warnings, competing accounts of earlier disclosures and public legal
      organising. This section separates those records by what was published
      and links each summary to the captured material. It does not turn them
      into a verdict or a procedure from this site.
      <Claim
        basis="verified"
        scope="That every destination in the response map below is backed by registered sources and held captures"
        source="The source register and individual evidence records maintained by this archive"
        href="/record/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <h2 id="map">Four parts of the response record</h2>

  <p>
    Start with the kind of publication you want to inspect. Each destination
    opens with a short answer, then gives the claims, limits and source links
    needed to check it.
  </p>

  <div class="response-map">
    <section class="response-map__card" id="vendor-operations">
      <p class="response-map__kicker">Statements</p>
      <h3><a href="/response/statements/">What organisations said and did</a></h3>
      <p>
        Coinkite's operational statements, custody-provider guidance, competing
        wallet vendors' responses, and community or organisational actions.
        These remain attributed statements unless the action is independently
        observable in the record.
      </p>
      <p class="response-map__links">
        <a href="/response/statements/#providers">Providers and wallet vendors</a>
        <a href="/response/statements/#coinkite">Coinkite's statements</a>
        <a id="community-response" href="/response/statements/#community">Community actions</a>
        <a id="data-retention" href="/response/statements/#outreach">Outreach and data retention</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="response-map__card" id="analyses">
      <p class="response-map__kicker">Technical work</p>
      <h3><a href="/response/developers/">What researchers and developers published</a></h3>
      <p>
        Pull requests, commit-history reconstruction and post-disclosure tests
        are kept apart from shipped fixes. The AI page separately records what
        is known, and not known, about the original discovery method.
      </p>
      <p class="response-map__links">
        <a href="/response/developers/">Developer proposals</a>
        <a href="/response/ai/">AI and discovery claims</a>
        <a href="/how-it-broke/blast-radius/#wizardsardine">Independent code analysis</a>
        <a href="/record/reference/#code">Published code register</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="response-map__card" id="provider-keys">
      <p class="response-map__kicker">Guidance and warnings</p>
      <h3><a href="/response/migration/">What publishers told readers</a></h3>
      <p>
        Migration recommendations and claimed outcomes are recorded as
        published guidance, not instructions from this archive. Documented scam
        artefacts are separated from warnings about what might happen.
      </p>
      <p class="response-map__links">
        <a href="/response/migration/">Migration guidance</a>
        <a href="/response/scams/">Scams and warnings</a>
        <a href="/how-it-broke/conditions/#multisig">Threshold-wallet conditions</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="response-map__card" id="prior-warnings">
      <p class="response-map__kicker">Accountability</p>
      <h3><a href="/response/disclosure-history/">What the competing records say</a></h3>
      <p>
        Coinkite's disclosure chronology is read alongside first-person and
        community accounts that do not fully agree with it. Terms, statutes,
        claimant organising and public legal disagreement are kept in a
        separate document register.
      </p>
      <p class="response-map__links">
        <a href="/response/disclosure-history/">Disclosure history and earlier-warning claims</a>
        <a href="/response/legal/">Terms and legal record</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 id="chronology">A short publication chronology</h2>

  <p>
    This is a route into the material, not a replacement for the full
    <a href="/record/timeline/">incident timeline</a>. Dates below identify when
    the selected responses were published; each link opens the held source
    record.
    <Claim
      basis="reported"
      scope="The publication dates and attributed response summaries in the four chronology entries below"
      source="The publishers' captured statements, articles and repository records, linked in each entry"
      href="/record/timeline/"
      compact
    />
  </p>

  <ol class="response-chronology">
    <li>
      <time datetime="2026-07-31">31 July</time>
      <div>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/unchained-guidance/">Custody providers publish migration guidance</a></h3>
        <p>Unchained and Casa describe key rotation and multi-vendor threshold policies; competing wallet vendors publish their own scope statements.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time datetime="2026-08-01">1 August</time>
      <div>
        <h3><a href="/response/developers/">Technical proposals and reconstructions appear</a></h3>
        <p>Upstream pull requests, independent commit reading and code-path analyses become part of the public record.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time datetime="2026-08-02">2 August</time>
      <div>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/">Coinkite publishes operational updates</a></h3>
        <p>The vendor reports shipment, stock, customer-outreach and migration-support actions, then acknowledges lasting damage.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time datetime="2026-08-04">4 August</time>
      <div>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/coinkite-historical-disclosures/">The vendor publishes a disclosure chronology</a></h3>
        <p>The chronology adds a new primary document to earlier-warning and AI-review disputes already in circulation.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <section class="response-boundary" id="precedent">
    <h2>What is not filed as a response</h2>
    <p>
      Earlier predictable-key incidents are background, not responses to this
      incident. They remain in the <a href="/record/reference/#analysis">reference
      register</a>. A Bitkey weakness published by Block on the same day was a
      different product and defect, so it is held only as an
      <a id="adjacent" href="/record/sources/bitkey-relationship-enrollment-report/">adjacent source record</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Article>

<style>
  .response-map {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    width: min(100cqi, 1000px);
    max-width: none;
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
  }
  .response-map__card {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.3rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 9rem;
  }
  .response-map__card h3 { margin-top: .35rem; font-size: 1.08rem; }
  .response-map__card > p:not(.response-map__kicker, .response-map__links) {
    margin-top: .55rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: .91rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .response-map__kicker {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: .64rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .response-map__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: .35rem .8rem;
    margin-top: .9rem;
    padding-top: .75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: .8rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .response-chronology {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1.2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: .15rem;
    max-width: var(--w-text);
  }
  .response-chronology li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.2rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .response-chronology li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .response-chronology time {
    padding-top: .15rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: .7rem;
    letter-spacing: .07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .response-chronology h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1rem; }
  .response-chronology p {
    margin-top: .35rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: .88rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .response-boundary {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--rule-2);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    max-width: var(--w-text);
    scroll-margin-top: 9rem;
  }
  .response-boundary h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .response-boundary p { margin-top: .55rem; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .response-map { grid-template-columns: 1fr; width: 100%; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .response-chronology li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: .35rem; }
  }
</style>
